### --- §0. Login ---
POST http://localhost:1538/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "admin",
  "password": "admin123"
}

### Paste the token from the response here:
@token = eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhZG1pbiIsImV4cCI6MTc4NTg1MjAzNSwic2NvcGVzIjpbIio6KjoqIl0sImp0aSI6IjY3MGI4MWQ0LTE3YzEtNGMzMS1iMzUxLTYxOGJhM2EwNjcwMSJ9.Ydxykz_rRuuDoocuE99YagiqFI2myoSAZ2EH3L7kAuk

###
### ============================================================
### COLLECTION-LEVEL TTL
###
### MoltenDB TTL operates at the collection level -- the entire
### collection is dropped in one O(1) call when the TTL elapses.
###
### SLIDING-WINDOW EXPIRY: the clock resets to (now + ttl_secs)
### at the end of every /set batch. A collection that keeps
### receiving inserts will never expire -- it only drops after
### ttl_secs of complete write inactivity.
###
### /update does NOT reset the clock. Only /set does.
###
### TTL is immutable -- once set it cannot be changed without
### dropping and recreating the collection.
###
### _expiresAt is a VIRTUAL field -- never stored in documents,
### computed from the collection TTL map and injected into every
### response. It is always returned when a TTL is registered.
### ============================================================

### §1. Register a collection-level TTL via /schema (no JSON schema required)
### The "cache" collection will be dropped 60 seconds after the last insert batch.
POST http://localhost:1538/schema
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "cache",
  "ttl": 60
}

### §2. Combine a collection-level TTL with a JSON schema
### The "sessions" collection expires 1800 seconds after the last insert batch.
### Both ttl and schema are optional -- either or both can be provided.
POST http://localhost:1538/schema
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "sessions",
  "schema": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "userId": {
        "type": "string"
      },
      "token": {
        "type": "string"
      }
    },
    "required": [
      "userId",
      "token"
    ]
  },
  "ttl": 60
}

### §3. Insert documents into a TTL collection
### The expiry clock resets to (now + 1800s) when this batch commits.
### Every document in the response will include "_expiresAt" (ISO 8601).
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "sessions",
  "data": {
    "sess_abc": {
      "userId": "u1",
      "token": "xyz123"
    },
    "sess_def": {
      "userId": "u2",
      "token": "abc456"
    }
  }
}

### §4. Register TTL inline on /set (shortcut -- no separate /schema call needed)
### Registers the TTL for the collection AND inserts documents in one request.
### The expiry clock resets to (now + 30s) when this batch commits.
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "cache",
  "data": {
    "hot_item": {
      "value": 42
    },
    "warm_item": {
      "value": 99
    }
  },
  "ttl": 30
}

### §5. Read a document -- _expiresAt is always returned as a virtual field
### Response includes "_expiresAt": "2026-..." computed from the collection TTL map.
### _expiresAt cannot be suppressed via fields or excludedFields.
POST http://localhost:1538/get
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "cache",
  "fields": [
    "value",
    "_expiresAt"
  ],
  "keys": "hot_item"
}

### §6. Inserting more documents resets the expiry clock from now
### After this insert, the collection expires 1800s from THIS moment -- not from §3.
### This is the sliding-window behaviour: active collections stay alive.
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "sessions",
  "data": {
    "sess_xyz": {
      "userId": "u3",
      "token": "def789"
    }
  }
}

### §7. Updating a document does NOT reset the expiry clock
### /update mutates existing documents but does not count as "activity"
### for TTL purposes -- the clock is unchanged after this call.
POST http://localhost:1538/update
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "sessions",
  "data": {
    "sess_abc": {
      "token": "refreshed_token"
    }
  }
}

### §8. Query with where -- _expiresAt is present on every result
### All standard where operators work normally on TTL collections.
POST http://localhost:1538/get
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "sessions",
  "where": {
    "userId": {
      "$eq": "u1"
    }
  },
  "fields": [
    "userId",
    "token"
  ]
}

### §9. Check that the entire collection is gone after the TTL elapses
### Wait for the TTL to pass, then run this -- expects 404 "No documents found".
### The entire collection is dropped in one O(1) call, not document by document.
POST http://localhost:1538/get
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "cache",
  "keys": [
    "hot_item",
    "warm_item"
  ]
}

### §10. Manual per-document expiry pattern (for OTPs, reset tokens, etc.)
### MoltenDB TTL is collection-level and not suitable for per-document expiry.
### For use cases like password resets or OTPs, store your own expiry timestamp
### in the document and use POST /delete with a where clause to clean up.

### §10a. Insert a document with a custom expiry timestamp (Unix ms)
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "password_resets",
  "data": {
    "token_abc": {
      "userId": "u1",
      "email": "user@example.com",
      "expires_at": 1747240200000
    }
  }
}

### §10b. Clean up expired entries -- delete all docs where expires_at is in the past
### Replace the value with Date.now() (Unix ms) from your client before sending.
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "password_resets",
  "where": {
    "expires_at": {
      "$lt": 1747240200000
    }
  }
}
